   "content": [
      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htAmericaAbyaYalaMsg\"></span> (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "160fb48c-1711-491e-a1aa-e1257e7889af",
                     "text": "Porto-Gonçalves, Carlos Walter",
                     "page": "pp. 39-43",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text continues the study of Roman numbers. Therefore, we suggest you read the previous text about their origin (<htcite0>) before continuing.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "886ccce8-3be8-4aba-bc86-94864e96b355",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Text or number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nWhen we learned about our numbers, we discovered that we have 10 symbols to represent decimal numbers (<htcite0>).\n\nOn the other hand, we saw that Etruscan-Roman numbers, also known as Latin numbers, have only 7 symbols. Among them, only two represent values from 1 to 9, and none of the seven appears in ascending sequence (<htcite1>).\n\nGiven this, let's reflect:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "648fb7b4-ba73-452e-980b-6d68d57d16b9",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Our current numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "886ccce8-3be8-4aba-bc86-94864e96b355",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Text or number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: And now?</b><br /><br />How would you represent the numbers we've used so far?<br /><br />Since Etruscan-Roman numbers don't have the same quantity of symbols as Indo-Arabic numbers, can we use them in the same way?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_like_there",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### The range from 0 to 10\n\nLet's divide the numbers from 1 to 9 into two groups to simplify understanding.\n\n#### The first 4 numbers\n\nSince the Etruscan-Roman number 1 (I) represents a unit mark (<htcite0>) and the next number to have a symbol is 5 (V), one of the ways found to represent them was repeating the units until reaching the next number, as shown in **Table 1** (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7be50fd2-2ce2-4d2d-b123-22d6bae50e23",
                     "text": "Keyser, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 541 - 543",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "28"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a372974f-4cea-4702-852d-40e70e155583",
                     "text": "Wheelock, Fredrick",
                     "page": "Chapter 15",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen tableTenBgBlue\"><tr><th>Numerical System</th><th  colspan=\"4\">Numbers</th></tr> <tr><td>Roman</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">I</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">II</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">III</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">IIII</td></tr>  <tr><td>Indo-Arabic</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">1</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">2</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">3</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">4</td></tr> <tr><td>Vigesimal Mesoamerican</td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt1\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt1', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 1\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_1.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt2\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt2', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 2\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_2.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt3\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt3', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 3\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_3.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt4\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt4', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 4\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_4.png\" /></td></tr></table> </p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Comparison of Roman, Indo-Arabic, and Mesoamerican numbers 1 to 4.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Notice that the logic of these Etruscan-Roman numbers follows an identical pattern to the numbers used in Mesoamerica (*Abya Yala*), despite the different format. This leads us to a new reflection:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: On origins</b><br /><br />Can we say that both numerical systems had the same origin?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Trying to create a single origin for the entire evolution of human knowledge is often tempting, but as the works of Francisco Barriga Puentes teach us, humanity created different numerical systems (<htcite0>). And, as other History Tracers texts show, the foundations for creating numbers and performing constructions are the same for all humanity, which, despite having a common origin, developed its numerical systems separately (<htcite1>; <htcite2>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ceffba8a-43be-4de3-a5c8-27cdf81311b1",
                     "text": "Puente, Francisco Barriga",
                     "page": "pp. 15 - 66",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2020",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "ab1dda1e-95c2-4087-8299-e851e58bdc6f",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Humans",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "08a1bd1a-a7b3-41f8-8656-01ad77a63171",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team (2026)",
                     "page": "What do I need to build?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "08",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Between 5 and 10\n\nSince the symbol 5 (V) is well defined in the Etruscan-Roman system, to form numbers between 5 and 9, we use it together with the unit counters already known (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a372974f-4cea-4702-852d-40e70e155583",
                     "text": "Wheelock, Fredrick",
                     "page": "Chapter 15",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen tableTenBgBlue\"><tr><th>Numerical System</th><th  colspan=\"5\">Numbers</th></tr> <tr><td>Roman</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">V</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">VI</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">VII</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">VIII</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">VIIII</td></tr>  <tr><td>Indo-Arabic</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">5</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">6</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">7</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">8</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">9</td></tr> <tr><td>Vigesimal Mesoamerican</td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt5\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt5', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 5\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_5.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt6\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt6', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 6\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_6.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt7\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt7', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 7\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_7.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt8\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt8', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 8\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_8.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt9\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt9', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 9\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_9.png\" /></td></tr></table> </p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 2</b>: Comparison of Roman, Indo-Arabic, and Mesoamerican (*Abya Yala*) numbers 5 to 9.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_before",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### What if it's placed before?\n\nSo far, we've seen that Etruscan-Roman numbers were built using the previous number as a base, adding it to the right. Now, let's reflect on another possibility.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: Let's invert</b><br /><br />What would happen if we reversed the logic and, instead of adding a number to the right of the previously defined number, we added a number before the next symbol in the sequence?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Although, when we live in a region, we learn an alphabet and how to write with it, it's not hard to find people who, using the same alphabet, write letters differently from what we know.\n\nIn the case of Etruscan-Roman numbers, this also happens for some numbers. For example, the numbers 4 and 9 were written in two different ways (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a372974f-4cea-4702-852d-40e70e155583",
                     "text": "Wheelock, Fredrick",
                     "page": "Chapter 15",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen tableTenBgBlue\"><tr><th>Numerical System</th><th  colspan=\"4\">Numbers</th></tr> <tr><td>Roman</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">IIII</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">IV</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">VIIII</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">IX</td></tr>  <tr><td>Indo-Arabic</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">4</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">4</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">9</td><td style=\"background-color: white;\">9</td></tr> <tr><td>Vigesimal Mesoamerican</td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt41\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt41', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 4\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_4.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt42\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt42', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 4\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_4.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt99\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt99', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 9\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_9.png\" /></td><td style=\"background-color: white; vertical-align: bottom;\"><img id=\"idt49\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('idt49', '0%')\" alt=\"Mayan numeral 9\" src=\"images/HistoryTracers/Maya_9.png\" /></td></tr></table> </p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 3</b>: Comparison of different versions of Etruscan-Roman numbers 4 and 9 with Indo-Arabic and Mesoamerican systems.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "As we can observe in **Table 3**, we can represent the number 4 and the number 9 with 4 unit symbols (I), or using just one. When we use just one, it precedes the next numerical symbol defined in the system: **V** for the number 5 and **X** for the number 10.\n\nIn Mesoamerica (*Abya Yala*), there was also freedom to write numbers in different ways. For example, in stelae texts — vertical monuments — like the one in **Figure 1**, Mesoamerican peoples could write the 4 dots of the 4 vertically instead of horizontally.",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": [],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgEstela\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgEstela', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\" style=\"width: 30%;\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Stela at the Museum of Anthropology of Guatemala City (<htcite0>), Guatemala. Photo taken on <htdate0></p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "a994054a-b530-47fe-bca1-259315f45684",
                     "text": "Museo Nacional de Arqueología y Etnología, Guatemala",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2014",
                     "month": "10",
                     "day": "21"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "In this image, there is a hieroglyphic text arranged in two columns.",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we learned that Etruscan-Roman numbers are written as follows:\n- Seven symbols are well defined and we always write them directly (<htcite0>).\n- For numbers from 1 to 4, we add unit values for each advance. The number 4 may be written as IIII (four unit values) or IV (the unit value to the left of the symbol for 5, V).\n- We do something similar for numbers up to 10, with the difference that, besides being able to have 5 (V) with four unit values (I), we will have the unit value before the symbol for 10 (X). The number 9 may be written as VIIII (5 plus four unit values) or IX (the unit value to the left of the symbol for 10, X).\n\nAdditionally, we compared the numbers developed in Mesoamerica (*Abya Yala*) and observed that, for the first 9 numbers, they have a similar logic, developed independently.\n\nFinally, we observed in these comparisons between isolated peoples, that they also developed distinct ways of writing their numbers.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "886ccce8-3be8-4aba-bc86-94864e96b355",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Text or number",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
